<h2>The question</h2>

<p>Web Everything holds an absolute rule: <strong>WE holds zero implementation</strong> — contract, protocol and
interface only, never delivery runtime, <em>"not even as a reference implementation"</em>
(<code>docs/agent/platform-decisions.md</code>, the <em>constellation-placement</em> anchor, rulings #1246/#1282).
Individual relocations have moved subsystem after subsystem out to Frontier UI. Decision
<a href="/backlog/1770-audit-the-end-state-constellation-placement-once-all-relocat/">#1770</a> was filed to confirm the
aggregate result: <em>is the line tight (A), or are there residuals (B)?</em></p>

<p>That is not a decidable question. Which branch obtains is a fact about the tree, settled by counting — nobody
<em>chooses</em> it. The real question sits one level up:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>What <strong>instrument</strong> confirms a zero-implementation boundary, and what keeps that confirmation true a
  month later — a codified placement map written into the statute, or a standing machine check?</p>
</blockquote>

<h2>Finding — the fork dissolves, and the answer is a NARROW instrument</h2>

<p><strong>The survey's conclusion, up front, so the rest reads in its light: the recommended instrument is
narrow and coverage-reporting — a new-path check over <code>blocks/</code> plus the nine named subsystem debt
roots, a re-pointed byte-parity gate on a hand-authored pair list, and a tripwire that errors when a check's
subject has vanished.</strong> It is
explicitly <em>not</em> a whole-tree classifier and <em>not</em> a regenerated canonical map. That broad shape was
drafted, run against the real repo, and <strong>rejected on measurement</strong> — see
<a href="#the-correction">the correction</a> below, which is a finding of this survey, not a caveat on it.</p>

<p>Two separate things dissolved. <strong>First, the doc-versus-check framing is a false either/or</strong> — the
prior art shows a map and a check can be one mechanism, which is why nobody has to choose. The two strongest
analogues — <a href="https://github.com/open-feature/spec">OpenFeature's specification repo</a> and
<a href="https://github.com/tc39/test262">TC39's test262</a> — do it like this:</p>

<ol>
  <li>a script walks <strong>every tracked path</strong> and classifies each against a declared ignore list;</li>
  <li>it <strong>regenerates a canonical map artifact</strong> from that classification;</li>
  <li>CI fails if the committed artifact differs from the regenerated one.</li>
</ol>

<p>OpenFeature's is <code>.specignore</code> + a markdown parser → <code>specification.json</code>, gated by
<code>git diff --name-only</code> in <code>pr-checks.yaml</code>. test262's is <code>./make.py</code> plus an
untracked-changes check. The transferable lesson is the <em>relationship</em> — the map is a projection of the
classifier, so it cannot go stale — <strong>not the scope</strong>. Both of those repos are homogeneous corpora
of spec text and test files; this repo is not, which is exactly what the measurement below shows.</p>

<p><strong>Second, "walk every tracked path" does not transfer.</strong> Run over this repo it red-gates the very
directories the inventory certifies as correctly placed, and it is green on both defects that motivate it. So the
mechanism carries over and the breadth does not: keep the projection relationship, shrink the subject to the
named debt roots.</p>

<p>The underlying principle is <a href="https://www.cs.ubc.ca/~murphy/papers/rm/fse95.html">Murphy, Notkin &amp;
Sullivan's Software Reflexion Models</a> (FSE-3, 1995; ACM SIGSOFT Retrospective Impact Paper Award), thirty years
early. Their opening claim is the whole argument: high-level models <em>"are almost always inaccurate with respect
to the system's source code."</em> Their fix is not a better document — it is to keep the map but <strong>demote
it from truth to hypothesis</strong> and let a tool compute where map and code diverge. Notably, a Reflexion Model
is scoped to a <em>declared subsystem map</em>, not to every file in the tree — the narrow reading is the faithful
one.</p>

<h2>Enforcement tooling — who actually fails on an unclassified file</h2>

<p>Most boundary tools are deny-lists: they ban known-bad edges. Only five surveyed ship a true
<strong>exhaustiveness</strong> mode, where a file or module matching nothing is itself an error.</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Ecosystem</th><th>Fail-closed?</th><th>What it checks</th><th>Failure mode</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><a href="https://import-linter.readthedocs.io/en/v2.9/contract_types/layers/">import-linter</a></td>
      <td>Python</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> — <code>exhaustive = true</code></td>
      <td>Layers / forbidden / independence contracts over the import graph</td>
      <td><code>exhaustive</code> is off by default; <code>exhaustive_ignores</code> is the hole</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><a href="https://www.jsboundaries.dev/docs/rules/no-unknown-files/">eslint-plugin-boundaries</a></td>
      <td>JS/TS</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> — <code>no-unknown-files</code></td>
      <td>Every <em>file</em> must classify into an element, then element→element import rules</td>
      <td>Anything under <code>boundaries/ignore</code> is never analysed</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><a href="https://www.archunit.org/userguide/html/000_Index.html">ArchUnit</a></td>
      <td>Java</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> — <code>ensureAllClassesAreContainedInArchitecture()</code></td>
      <td>Class/package deps, layers, slices, cycles</td>
      <td>Only sees classes in the test's scope; the same PR can edit the rule</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><a href="https://nx.dev/docs/features/enforce-module-boundaries">Nx enforce-module-boundaries</a></td>
      <td>JS/TS monorepo</td><td><strong>Yes by construction</strong> — an untagged project may depend on nothing</td>
      <td>Tag→tag dependency constraints</td>
      <td>Defeated by the documented <code>sourceTag: '*'</code> "brute way"</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><a href="https://github.com/sverweij/dependency-cruiser">dependency-cruiser</a></td>
      <td>JS/TS</td><td>Optional — <code>allowed</code> + <code>not-in-allowed</code></td>
      <td>Module dependency graph edges; orphans; required</td>
      <td><code>allowedSeverity</code> defaults to <code>warn</code>, so the allow-list passes CI silently</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Go internal packages</td><td>Go</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> — compiler-enforced, zero config</td>
      <td>Import graph</td><td>One positional axis only; no tags or layers</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><a href="https://bazel.build/concepts/visibility">Bazel visibility</a></td>
      <td>Polyglot</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> — default <code>//visibility:private</code></td>
      <td>Target-level access across packages</td>
      <td>Load visibility defaults <em>public</em>; a package <code>default_visibility</code> reopens everything</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>JPMS · Rust visibility · .NET <code>InternalsVisibleTo</code></td>
      <td>Java / Rust / .NET</td><td><strong>Yes</strong> — language-level default-deny</td>
      <td>Module / item / assembly visibility</td>
      <td>Reflection and classpath escape hatches; single structural axis</td>
    </tr>
