</table>

<p>The pattern worth naming: <strong>default-deny is the language norm and the lint exception.</strong> Where a
compiler owns the boundary (Rust, Go, Bazel, JPMS, .NET) fail-closed is free and unarguable. Where a config file owns
it, fail-closed is opt-in — and every such tool ships a catch-all that turns it off.</p>

<h2>Allow-list versus deny-list</h2>

<p>The principle is not in dispute. OWASP's positive security model holds that deny-lists are
<em>"easy to bypass"</em> because badness is easier to vary than goodness is to define. For a boundary rule the
argument is sharper than for input validation: <strong>a deny-list of known-bad paths cannot cover a file that does
not exist yet</strong>, and the file that does not exist yet is the entire point of a standing check.</p>

<p>The adoption cost is real and has a known answer: <strong>a ratchet</strong>. ArchUnit's
<code>FreezingArchRule</code> baselines existing violations to a store and <em>"will automatically reduce the known
stored violations to prevent any regression"</em>; dependency-cruiser has <code>--output-type baseline</code>;
<a href="https://eslint.org/docs/latest/use/suppressions">ESLint bulk suppressions</a> is the best-designed of the
family because it errors on <em>stale</em> suppressions too, so a baseline cannot quietly outlive the violations it
excused.</p>

<p>Every failure in the survey traces to one design detail — <strong>the escape hatch</strong>. Nx's
<code>sourceTag: '*'</code>, Sheriff's <code>root: ['*']</code> default, <code>exhaustive_ignores</code>,
codeowners-validator's <code>ALLOW_UNOWNED_PATTERNS=true</code> default, dependency-cruiser's <code>warn</code>
severity. Dan Abramov's <a href="https://overreacted.io/suppressions-of-suppressions/">Suppressions of
Suppressions</a> names the social mechanism: mass-injected ignores <em>"normalize the practice of suppressing lint
rules, and if people get used to seeing them everywhere, nobody will notice when humans suppress the rules for new
code."</em> An exact-match, monotonically-shrinking exceptions file survives; a wildcard does not.</p>

<p>That "every tracked file must classify" scales is settled by <a href="https://reuse.software/spec-3.3/">REUSE</a>,
which requires copyright and licence metadata on every file and is CI-linted at Linux-kernel scale. The kernel case is
directly on point: they ran the one-off inventory (the mass SPDX conversion) <em>and then</em> added the standing
per-file check — <strong>the check is what stops the inventory from being undone.</strong></p>

<h2>How standards bodies keep a spec repo free of implementation</h2>

<p><strong>No standards body has a written "this repo holds no implementation" policy.</strong> Searched across WPT,
WHATWG, W3C, OpenFeature, OpenAPI, JSON Schema and test262 — it does not exist as prose anywhere. What exists is
structure and, in two cases, a machine check.</p>

<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Body</th><th>Explicit "no impl" text</th><th>Machine check</th><th>Fail-closed?</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>web-platform-tests</td><td>No — but carries the only <em>named</em> prohibition found anywhere: a
      <code>MojomJSFile</code> rule, <em>"Don't check *.mojom.js files into WPT"</em></td>
      <td><code>wpt lint --all</code>, 8 blocklist rules</td><td>Whole tree visited, deny-list semantics</td></tr>
    <tr><td>WHATWG</td><td>No — its stated principle runs the other way (<em>"don't write fiction"</em>: ensure
      implementations follow the standard and vice versa)</td><td>None</td><td>Open</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>OpenFeature</strong></td><td>Implied, normative — the SDK <em>"does not itself handle the flag
      evaluation logic"</em></td><td><strong><code>.specignore</code> + parser + <code>git diff</code>
      gate</strong></td><td><strong>Closed on <code>.md</code></strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>OpenAPI</td><td>Closest written sentence: npm scripts are <em>"intentionally thin wrappers"</em>; tooling
      moved out to a separate repo</td><td>markdownlint, schema tests</td><td>Open</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>test262</strong></td><td>No (implied)</td><td><strong>13-check per-file classifier + mandatory
      YAML frontmatter + an exact-match <code>--exceptions</code> file</strong></td>
      <td><strong>Closed on changed <code>.js</code>/<code>.json</code></strong></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>OpenFeature is the closest analogue to the Web Everything constellation: a spec repo that declares the boundary
normatively, requires conforming SDKs to track a spec version <em>"not less than 2 minor versions behind the
latest"</em>, and is consumed as a git submodule by every SDK. Its honest carve-out is instructive — the spec repo
<em>does</em> contain Python and JavaScript under <code>tools/</code>. It holds zero <em>runtime</em> implementation,
not zero code. <strong>"Zero implementation" only becomes enforceable once you write down what the exceptions
are.</strong></p>

<h2>Why a placement map does not belong in a decision record</h2>

<p>Codifying a current-state inventory into a ratified document is not merely fragile — it is a <strong>named
anti-pattern</strong>.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Nygard's original ADR post</strong> (2011): <em>"Large documents are never kept up to date… Nobody ever
  reads large documents, either."</em> An ADR is <em>designed to survive becoming false</em> — superseded, not
  edited. An inventory is not.</li>
  <li><strong>Microsoft Well-Architected</strong>: <em>"The ADR serves as an append-only log. Don't go back and edit
  accepted records."</em> And directly: <em>"Avoid making decision records design guides."</em></li>
  <li><strong>Olaf Zimmermann</strong> (adr.github.io) names two anti-patterns that describe a placement map exactly:
  <strong>"Mega-ADR"</strong> — detailed architecture information stuffed into multi-page records serving as
  documentation monster — and <strong>"Blueprint or Policy in Disguise"</strong>.</li>
  <li><strong>C4</strong>: Simon Brown grades artifacts by decay rate. Context and container diagrams change slowly
  and can be hand-maintained; code-level diagrams <em>"will potentially become outdated very quickly."</em> His remedy
  for the fast-decaying levels is <em>generation</em> — because generating them <em>"will ensure they are kept up to
  date and reflect reality at all times."</em></li>
</ul>

<p>The empirical evidence is Lethbridge, Singer &amp; Forward, <em>How Software Engineers Use Documentation</em> (IEEE
Software 20(6), 2003). 68% agreed documentation is always outdated relative to the system; engineers consulted
documentation in only 3% of observed events. But the decisive detail is their correlation table: <strong>the closer a
document sits to the code, the more its accuracy governs whether anyone uses it</strong> (testing 0.67, low-level
design 0.58, architectural 0.41, specifications 0.03). A per-path placement map is the closest-to-code architecture
document there is — the worst possible position on that curve.</p>

<p>On human review as the alternative: Li, Liang, Soliman &amp; Avgeriou's
<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.01184">OpenStack code-review study</a> found that across <strong>21,274</strong>
review comments in Nova and Neutron, only <strong>502</strong> discussed architecture-erosion symptoms. Their
<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.11392">practitioner study</a> (ICPC 2021) concludes that dedicated erosion
detection tools are lacking.</p>

<h2>Fitness functions — what the literature actually supports</h2>

<p>Ford, Parsons &amp; Kua define an architectural fitness function as <em>"any mechanism that provides an objective
integrity assessment of some architectural characteristic(s)"</em>; the second edition's subtitle changed to
<em>Automated Software Governance</em>. Rebecca Parsons' precision test is the useful one here: <em>"cyclomatic
complexity of less than five can be a fitness function"</em> — as opposed to a directive like "be maintainable".
<strong>A rule that can only be judged is not a fitness function.</strong> "The standards repo holds zero runtime
implementation" passes that test only if you can write the classifier.</p>

<p><strong>Three corrections to the folk version of this argument</strong>, worth stating because they are commonly
mis-cited:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>"Continual beats triggered" misreads the taxonomy.</strong> In the book, <em>triggered</em> means
  event-driven (a test on commit/merge/deploy) and <em>continual</em> means always-on production monitoring. A CI
  classifier is <em>triggered</em>. The axis that carries the argument is <strong>automated versus manual</strong>,
  not continual versus triggered.</li>
  <li><strong>The technique never reached "Adopt" on the ThoughtWorks Radar.</strong> "Architectural fitness function"
  entered at Trial (Nov 2017), stayed Trial (May 2018), and dropped off. The two that reached Adopt are narrow and
  concrete: <em>dependency drift fitness function</em> and <em>run cost as architecture fitness function</em>.
  ArchUnit itself went Assess → Trial. The pattern is that specific instantiations were adopted, not the general
  idea.</li>
  <li><strong>The book does not say "start manual, automate later."</strong> It is in fact more moderate than its
  reputation: <em>"Don't mistake the function part of our definition as implying that architects must express all
  fitness functions in code."</em></li>
</ul>

<p>The fair critique, from Ben Morris: <em>"you tend to get what you measure. Fitness functions based on metrics can
very quickly become perceived as targets."</em> A green check becomes evidence of health it cannot actually
provide.</p>

<p>The bridge between the two artifacts already exists in the literature. Richards &amp; Ford
(<em>Fundamentals of Software Architecture</em>, ch. 19) add a <strong>Compliance</strong> section to the ADR
template, stating whether a decision can be verified by an automated fitness function and how. That makes the
document <em>a specification of how the decision will be enforced</em> — not a competing description of state.</p>

<h2>What this means for Web Everything</h2>

<p><strong>The in-repo precedent already ruled the identical question on the sibling axis.</strong> Decision
<a href="/backlog/2006-differentiate-we-the-standard-from-we-the-website-app-make-t/">#2006</a> found the WE-website a
mis-homed product under the same placement rule, and
<a href="/backlog/2052-lift-the-we-website-render-under-a-site-boundary-add-the-fai/">#2052</a> shipped its carrier:
a fail-closed classifier in <code>check:standards</code> where a path in the zone matching neither surface set is a
hard error. Its fork-existence justification was <em>"a glob manifest and a directory boundary are mutually exclusive
mechanics for the same classification — maintaining both is redundant drift."</em></p>

<p><strong>The impl axis has no such carrier.</strong> Grepping the repo's scripts, git hooks and CI workflows for the
zero-implementation ruling's own lineage (<code>1282</code>, <code>1246</code>, <code>zero-impl</code>, "delivery
runtime", "WE-resident") over non-test <code>.mjs</code> sources returns <strong>7 hits</strong>: five are prose
comments that assert the rule without testing anything, and two belong to <code>guard-backward-edge.mjs</code> — its
file header and a live deny-message string in an <em>enforcing</em> hook. So the honest claim is not "no code
mentions the rule". It is that <strong>none of the seven enforces the residency clause</strong>: the five cannot,
and the hook enforces a <em>different</em> clause (it denies a static <code>@frontierui</code> import) at a scope
documented as WE's own <code>src/**</code> — which excludes the entire <code>blocks/</code> subtree where the
residual runtime actually lives, including the ten files there that already carry that very import. The operative
clause — <em>"no <strong>new</strong> WE-resident delivery runtime may be added"</em> — is held by model judgment
alone.</p>

<p>And the cost of that is already measurable. The decision card's own lineage records a constellation-wide hand
inventory on 2026-06-24 that explicitly <em>cleared</em> the gen-wrapper fixture. Eight weeks later
<code>we:scripts/gen-wrapper/genWrapper.mjs</code> stands at <strong>228 lines</strong> against
<code>fui:tools/gen-wrapper/genWrapper.mjs</code>'s <strong>417</strong>, missing an entire feature system — and at
directory scale the gap is wider still: <strong>437 tracked lines across 3 files</strong> in WE against
<strong>1,270 across 11</strong> in Frontier UI, which additionally carries a surface-contract module, a wrapper
form catalog, a template emitter and three type-declaration files that WE has no counterpart for. Sharper still:
that fixture was sanctioned as a "reference fixture" on 2026-06-18, and the ruling that withdrew the
reference-implementation tier <em>wholesale</em> landed two days later, on 2026-06-20. <strong>It survived its own
repeal</strong> — because a ruling changes a rule but enumerates nothing.</p>

<h2 id="the-correction">The correction — why the broad instrument is the wrong lesson</h2>

<p>The obvious reading of everything above is "so widen the classifier to the whole tree". That reading was drafted,
executed against the real repo, and <strong>fails</strong>. Run over all 6,980 tracked paths it produces
<strong>774 hard errors</strong> — including the contracts, conformance-vector, capability-manifest and webcases
directories that the same inventory certifies as correctly placed — and it is <strong>green on both defects that
motivate it</strong>, because a path classifier cannot see file <em>content</em> and cannot see the sibling repo.
The gen-wrapper divergence is FUI growing while WE stands still; no path-based rule will ever notice it. #2052's own
source comment, in the file usually cited as the precedent, records the whole-tree widening as considered and
rejected: classifying every tracked path <em>"would be noise and would red-gate the whole repo."</em></p>

<p><strong>And the deeper correction: the recurring defect is not stale prose.</strong> The tempting inference —
<em>prose rots, code stays true</em> — is false in this repo, which contains <strong>two machine checks that have
already rotted into no-ops</strong>. A cross-repo byte-parity gate went vacuous when its subject directory was
deleted; a codegen-placement invariant went dead when its subject file was deleted. Both are guarded by an
existence check, so both report success while covering nothing. Together with the fixture that outlived its repeal
and an epic whose four filed slices covered four of sixteen named targets, that is <strong>four instances of one
failure mode: an instrument that silently stops covering anything.</strong></p>

<p>The lesson is therefore the opposite of "make the check broader". It is <strong>make the check narrow, and make
it report its own coverage</strong> — a check that cannot say <em>"I currently cover N paths, down from M"</em> is
one deletion away from decorative. That, plus re-pointing the byte-parity gate that already exists at the duplicate
set that actually drifted, is what the prior art's generator-plus-diff-gate shape buys here.</p>

<p><strong>One structural caveat.</strong> A path classifier can only classify paths in the repo it runs in. In a
multi-repo constellation, the <em>direction</em> of dependency is enforced structurally by package publishing — that
is free. But "this repo contains zero runtime implementation" is a <strong>within-repo</strong> property, and nothing
about the repo split enforces it. The split gives you the arrow; only a within-repo classifier gives you the
emptiness. That asymmetry is the concrete reason a standing check is not redundant with the architecture.</p>

